<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali ibn Muhammad al-Sulayhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1067</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali ibn Muhammad al-Sulayhi was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulayhid-dynasty">Sulayhid dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, which ruled in the name of the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Proclaiming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> rule in 1047, he conquered most of Yemen including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sana'a</a> and extended influence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. He was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-al-ahwal">Sa'id al-Ahwal</a> of the Najahid dynasty, whose father he had poisoned.</p></div>
